BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 575.2 Tm (1. A patient has a sudden rise in arteriolar sympathetic firing with minimal change in cardiac output. Which) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 561.5 Tm (immediate variable rises most directly?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 544.8 Tm (Answer: C. Total peripheral resistance.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 528.5 Tm (Explanation: Arteriolar constriction increases resistance and therefore tends to raise arterial pressure even at) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 515.4 Tm (stable output.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 499.3 Tm (Not A: SA nodal rate concerns cardiac autonomic effects rather than arteriolar action.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 485.9 Tm (Not B: Metabolite clearance depends on local blood flow, not this direct action.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 472.5 Tm (Not D: Sympathetic venoconstriction generally reduces venous compliance.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 459.1 Tm (Source: circulation-2:Q27 | 3, 13, 19, 21) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 435.9 Tm (2. The R-R separation is four large squares on a regular standard-speed ECG. What is the heart rate?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 419.2 Tm (Answer: A. 75 beats/min.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 402.9 Tm (Explanation: At 25 mm/s, a large square equals 0.20 s. Four large squares represent 0.8 s per beat, giving 60/0.8 = 75) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 389.8 Tm (beats/min.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 373.7 Tm (Not B: 120 beats/min would require 2.5 large squares.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 360.3 Tm (Not C: 60 beats/min would require five large squares between R waves.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 346.9 Tm (Not D: 90 beats/min would require about 3.33 large squares.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 333.5 Tm (Source: lecture-7:Q42 | 13-14) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 310.3 Tm (3. An ECG develops a new shift of the ST segment above or below the baseline. Which process specifically) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 296.6 Tm (makes this segment clinically important?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 279.9 Tm (Answer: A. Myocardial ischaemia or inadequate oxygenation can alter ST level.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 263.6 Tm (Explanation: Ventricular ischaemia or hypoxia can disturb membrane potentials and cause ST elevation or) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 250.5 Tm (depression, so the segment is useful in recognising myocardial injury patterns.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 234.4 Tm (Not B: Atrial depolarisation is the P wave and normally precedes ST.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 221.0 Tm (Not C: Preload is a filling variable and does not directly set ST height.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 207.6 Tm (Not D: ST displacement is an electrical finding, not the normal marker of valve closure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 194.2 Tm (Source: lecture-7:Q27 | 9) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 171.0 Tm (4. Portal hypertension promotes ascites partly by increasing which force?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 154.3 Tm (Answer: B. Hydrostatic pressure in hepatic sinusoids.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 138.0 Tm (Explanation: Higher sinusoidal hydrostatic pressure increases fluid filtration and hepatic lymph formation; excess) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 124.9 Tm (can enter the peritoneal cavity.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 108.8 Tm (Not A: Higher plasma oncotic pressure would oppose outward fluid filtration.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 95.4 Tm (Not C: Improved lymphatic clearance would tend to oppose accumulation.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 82.0 Tm (Not D: Oxygen tension is not the main Starling-force mechanism.) Tj ET
