q .08 .14 .26 rg 0 792 595 50 re f Q
BT /F2 12.0 Tf 1 1 1 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 815.0 Tm (DOMiNANCE / CVS PHYSIOLOGY) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 1 1 1 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 798.0 Tm (MOCK 04 | 60Q | EXPLANATION GUIDE | 90 MIN) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 745.0 Tm (Source: lecture-6:Q44 | 37) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 721.8 Tm (5. Which contrast best distinguishes forward failure from backward failure?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 705.1 Tm (Answer: A. Forward reduces delivery; backward causes venous congestion.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 688.8 Tm (Explanation: Forward failure describes inadequate delivery to downstream tissues, whereas backward failure) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 675.7 Tm (describes elevated pressure and congestion upstream of a failing chamber.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 659.6 Tm (Not B: The two effects are reversed in this statement.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 646.2 Tm (Not C: Reduced flow and congestion, not excessive output, are the defining patterns.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 632.8 Tm (Not D: Either side can produce both forward and backward consequences.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 619.4 Tm (Source: circulation-3:Q48 | Circulation 3, slides 32-33) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 596.2 Tm (6. After severe head injury, a patient has high blood pressure, a slow pulse and irregular breathing. What) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 582.5 Tm (does this combination suggest?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 565.8 Tm (Answer: B. A dangerous Cushing response to intracranial compression.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 549.5 Tm (Explanation: Hypertension, reflex bradycardia and abnormal respiration form the Cushing triad, a late sign of) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 536.4 Tm (severe intracranial pressure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 520.3 Tm (Not A: Exercise more commonly raises both rate and output.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 506.9 Tm (Not C: Local muscle dilation does not explain the full neurological triad.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 493.5 Tm (Not D: Atrial overfilling usually causes tachycardia in the lecture's account.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 480.1 Tm (Source: circulation-2:Q56 | 32-36) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 456.9 Tm (7. What directly promotes pulmonary oedema in left-sided backward heart failure?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 440.2 Tm (Answer: C. Raised pulmonary venous and capillary pressure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 423.9 Tm (Explanation: Left ventricular failure increases left atrial and pulmonary venous pressures, driving filtration) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 410.8 Tm (into the lungs.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 394.7 Tm (Not A: Lower capillary hydrostatic pressure would oppose filtration.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 381.3 Tm (Not B: Systemic venous pressure is more closely linked to right-sided failure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 367.9 Tm (Not D: Right atrial pressure alone does not explain pulmonary oedema.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 354.5 Tm (Source: circulation-3:Q52 | Circulation 3, slide 33; Chapter 21 ?3) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 331.3 Tm (8. Why does the fetal cardiovascular system divert much blood away from the pulmonary circuit before birth?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 314.6 Tm (Answer: D. The placenta oxygenates blood; fetal lungs resist flow.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 298.3 Tm (Explanation: The placenta performs gas exchange before birth; fluid-filled fetal lungs have high resistance,) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 285.2 Tm (favouring right-to-left shunts.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 269.1 Tm (Not A: The fetal left ventricle contributes significantly to systemic output.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 255.7 Tm (Not B: Pulmonary resistance is high before aeration.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 242.3 Tm (Not C: Umbilical arteries carry relatively deoxygenated blood toward placenta.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 228.9 Tm (Source: lecture-6:Q53 | 42-44; Chapter 27 ?1) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 205.7 Tm (9. After bleeding stops, both haemoglobin and plasma-protein concentrations fall as circulating volume) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 192.0 Tm (recovers. What explains this?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 175.3 Tm (Answer: A. Added fluid dilutes the remaining blood components.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 159.0 Tm (Explanation: Fluid recruited from tissues or given intravenously restores volume before red-cell and protein mass) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 145.9 Tm (recover, producing dilution.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 129.8 Tm (Not B: New red-cell production raises rather than lowers haemoglobin mass.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 116.4 Tm (Not C: Renal filtration is generally depressed during acute severe shock.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 103.0 Tm (Not D: Venoconstriction redistributes blood rather than removing haemoglobin.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 89.6 Tm (Source: circulation-3:Q35 | Circulation 3, slides 24) Tj ET
