q .08 .14 .26 rg 0 792 595 50 re f Q
BT /F2 12.0 Tf 1 1 1 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 815.0 Tm (DOMiNANCE / CVS PHYSIOLOGY) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 1 1 1 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 798.0 Tm (MOCK 04 | 60Q | EXPLANATION GUIDE | 90 MIN) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 745.0 Tm (10. An ECG electrode detects a voltage on the skin even though the impulse originates in the myocardium. What) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 731.3 Tm (makes this possible?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 714.6 Tm (Answer: C. Cardiac electrical currents spread through conducting body tissues.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 698.3 Tm (Explanation: Depolarisation and repolarisation create voltage differences that propagate through the conductive) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 685.2 Tm (tissues of the body and can be detected at the skin.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 669.1 Tm (Not A: Mechanical pulsation is distinct from electrical potential recorded by ECG electrodes.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 655.7 Tm (Not B: Surface ECG electrodes do not directly measure intravascular blood pressure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 642.3 Tm (Not D: The electrodes sit on intact skin, not within the myocardium.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 628.9 Tm (Source: lecture-7:Q1 | 2) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 605.7 Tm (11. Heart rate rises from 70 to 80 beats/min while stroke volume stays at 70 mL. By how much does cardiac) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 592.0 Tm (output increase?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 575.3 Tm (Answer: B. By 0.70 litres per minute.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 559.0 Tm (Explanation: The increase is 10 beats/min x 70 mL/beat = 700 mL/min, or 0.70 L/min.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 542.9 Tm (Not A: 0.80 L/min treats the extra beats as a larger volume.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 529.5 Tm (Not C: 0.49 L/min does not follow from the given rate change.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 516.1 Tm (Not D: 1.40 L/min doubles the actual change.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 502.7 Tm (Source: circulation-2:Q20 | 1, 3) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 479.5 Tm (12. At unchanged blood density, vessel diameter and viscosity, what happens to Reynolds number if average) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 465.8 Tm (blood velocity doubles?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 449.1 Tm (Answer: A. A twofold increase.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 432.8 Tm (Explanation: Reynolds number is proportional to blood velocity when density, viscosity and vessel diameter remain) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 419.7 Tm (fixed. Doubling velocity therefore doubles Reynolds number and makes turbulence more likely.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 403.6 Tm (Not B: A halving would require the velocity-dependent term to decrease.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 390.2 Tm (Not C: Velocity enters the Reynolds relation linearly, not as its square.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 376.8 Tm (Not D: Reynolds number changes when velocity changes at fixed other variables.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 363.4 Tm (Source: circulation-1:Q54 | 65-66) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 340.2 Tm (13. Most sympathetic constrictor fibres release noradrenaline onto arteriolar smooth muscle. Which receptor) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 326.5 Tm (mainly mediates constriction?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 309.8 Tm (Answer: D. The alpha-1 adrenergic receptor.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 293.5 Tm (Explanation: Noradrenaline binds alpha1 receptors on arteriolar smooth muscle, raising intracellular Ca^2+ and) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 280.4 Tm (contracting the muscle. This raises resistance and arterial pressure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 264.3 Tm (Not A: beta2 activation typically dilates muscle arterioles.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 250.9 Tm (Not B: M2 receptors mediate vagal slowing of the heart.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 237.5 Tm (Not C: H2 receptors respond to histamine, which dilates arterioles.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 224.1 Tm (Source: circulation-2:Q25 | 13-14, 19) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 200.9 Tm (14. A harsh systolic murmur develops during blood ejection through a narrowed aortic valve. What mechanism) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 187.2 Tm (explains it?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 170.5 Tm (Answer: B. Turbulent forward flow through a stenotic outflow orifice.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 154.2 Tm (Explanation: Aortic stenosis accelerates blood through a narrow outlet during systole, causing a turbulent ejection) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 141.1 Tm (murmur.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 125.0 Tm (Not A: The aortic valve opens when ventricular pressure exceeds aortic pressure in systole.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 111.6 Tm (Not C: Mitral regurgitation usually occurs during systole, not diastole.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 98.2 Tm (Not D: Laminar flow through normal valves ordinarily produces no such murmur.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 84.8 Tm (Source: circulation-1:Q32 | 31-33; Chapter 5 ?7) Tj ET
