BT /F2 12.0 Tf 1 1 1 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 815.0 Tm (DOMiNANCE / CVS PHYSIOLOGY) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 1 1 1 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 798.0 Tm (MOCK 04 | 60Q | EXPLANATION GUIDE | 90 MIN) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 745.0 Tm (15. What distinguishes the normal x descent from the y descent in a JVP trace?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 728.3 Tm (Answer: A. x: atrial relaxation; y: emptying through the open tricuspid.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 712.0 Tm (Explanation: The x descent reflects atrial relaxation and downward tricuspid movement; the y descent follows) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 698.9 Tm (tricuspid opening as blood empties rapidly.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 682.8 Tm (Not B: Atrial contraction creates the a wave.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 669.4 Tm (Not C: Both descents reflect right atrial pressure changes rather than the proposed events.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 656.0 Tm (Not D: Left-heart valve events do not define these JVP descents.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 642.6 Tm (Source: circulation-1:Q70 | 24-25; Chapter 11 ?4) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 619.4 Tm (16. At 25 mm/s, a PR interval extends across 4.5 small squares. Which interpretation is most accurate?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 602.7 Tm (Answer: D. 0.18 s, inside the stated PR-interval range.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 586.4 Tm (Explanation: The horizontal duration is 4.5 x 0.04 = 0.18 s. This lies within the 0.12-0.20 s interval given in the) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 573.3 Tm (lecture.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 557.2 Tm (Not A: This is not the product of 4.5 and 0.04 s.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 543.8 Tm (Not B: This incorrectly halves the duration obtained from 4.5 small squares.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 530.4 Tm (Not C: This incorrectly assigns 0.20 s to each small square.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 517.0 Tm (Source: lecture-7:Q37 | 7, 13) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 493.8 Tm (17. Why does fetal haemoglobin load oxygen effectively at the placental interface?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 477.1 Tm (Answer: B. Its weaker 2,3-BPG binding increases oxygen affinity.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 460.8 Tm (Explanation: HbF interacts less with 2,3-BPG, shifting its oxygen dissociation curve left and favouring oxygen) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 447.7 Tm (uptake from maternal blood.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 431.6 Tm (Not A: Stronger 2,3-BPG binding would lower oxygen affinity.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 418.2 Tm (Not C: Fetal haemoglobin still contains iron-bearing haem groups.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 404.8 Tm (Not D: Oxygen binding is reversible, not permanent.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 391.4 Tm (Source: lecture-6:Q58 | 45) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 368.2 Tm (18. A patient develops headache that is worse upright after lumbar CSF sampling. Which mechanism is most) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 354.5 Tm (likely?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 337.8 Tm (Answer: A. Persistent CSF leakage lowers intracranial CSF pressure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 321.5 Tm (Explanation: Leakage from the dural puncture can reduce CSF pressure and cause a characteristic postural headache.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 305.4 Tm (Not B: BBB disruption is not the usual cause of post-lumbar-puncture headache.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 292.0 Tm (Not C: Production does not normally surge to cause this symptom.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 278.6 Tm (Not D: The foramina are far from the lumbar puncture site.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 265.2 Tm (Source: lecture-6:Q11 | 17; Chapter 22 ?5) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 242.0 Tm (19. During severe hepatic hypoperfusion, cells near the central vein are especially vulnerable to hypoxia.) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 228.3 Tm (Why?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 211.6 Tm (Answer: C. They are farthest downstream from the portal triads.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 195.3 Tm (Explanation: Centrilobular cells receive blood after it has travelled from portal triads, making them less) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 182.2 Tm (oxygenated and vulnerable.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 166.1 Tm (Not A: Centrilobular hepatocytes remain within the sinusoidal perfusion system.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 152.7 Tm (Not B: Oxygenated hepatic arterial inflow enters near the portal triads.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 139.3 Tm (Not D: Blood oxygen content generally falls as it traverses sinusoids.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 125.9 Tm (Source: lecture-6:Q41 | 35) Tj ET
