BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 745.0 Tm (Source: circulation-3:Q13 | Chapter 19 ?4 \(supplementary\)) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 721.8 Tm (25. In severe haemorrhagic shock, why does sympathetic constriction of splanchnic vessels help initially?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 705.1 Tm (Answer: D. It redistributes flow and mobilises stored venous blood.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 688.8 Tm (Explanation: The splanchnic bed is a large blood reservoir; vasoconstriction redirects perfusion and recruits) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 675.7 Tm (stored blood toward central circulation.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 659.6 Tm (Not A: Mucosal flow may decrease, risking ischaemia.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 646.2 Tm (Not B: Extraction is not necessarily complete.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 632.8 Tm (Not C: Severe or prolonged constriction can contribute to intestinal injury.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 619.4 Tm (Source: lecture-6:Q43 | 36) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 596.2 Tm (26. An examination reveals raised JVP, a tender enlarged liver, ascites and ankle oedema. Which haemodynamic) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 582.5 Tm (change unifies these?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 565.8 Tm (Answer: A. High systemic venous pressure behind the right ventricle.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 549.5 Tm (Explanation: Right ventricular backward failure elevates right atrial and systemic venous pressures, causing) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 536.4 Tm (jugular distension, hepatic congestion and dependent oedema.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 520.3 Tm (Not B: Higher systemic venous pressure promotes, rather than reduces, filtration.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 506.9 Tm (Not C: Isolated pulmonary venous congestion principally reflects left-sided failure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 493.5 Tm (Not D: Right-sided backward failure increases rather than decreases venous pressure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 480.1 Tm (Source: circulation-3:Q56 | Circulation 3, slides 33) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 456.9 Tm (27. An isolated SA node fires at about 100 beats/min, whereas the same person's resting pulse was about 70.) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 443.2 Tm (Which interpretation best fits?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 426.5 Tm (Answer: C. Resting vagal activity had restrained SA nodal firing.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 410.2 Tm (Explanation: With no nerve input the SA node fires at about 100 beats/min. At rest the vagus releases acetylcholine) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 397.1 Tm (continuously \(vagal tone\), holding the rate down to about 60-80. Blocking the vagus, for example with atropine, lets) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 384.0 Tm (the rate rise.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 367.9 Tm (Not A: Atrial conduction is not the main cause of the slower intrinsic SA pacemaker rate.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 354.5 Tm (Not B: The isolated rate shows the pacemaker is capable of firing faster.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 341.1 Tm (Not D: Sympathetic activity usually increases SA nodal firing.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 327.7 Tm (Source: circulation-2:Q30 | 20) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 304.5 Tm (28. Immediately after the first effective breaths, what happens to pulmonary vascular resistance?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 287.8 Tm (Answer: D. It falls as the lungs expand and oxygen tension rises.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 271.5 Tm (Explanation: Lung aeration and increased alveolar oxygen rapidly lower pulmonary vascular resistance, increasing) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 258.4 Tm (pulmonary blood flow.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 242.3 Tm (Not A: Alveolar oxygen generally relieves pulmonary hypoxic vasoconstriction.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 228.9 Tm (Not B: Pulmonary adaptation begins with the first breaths.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 215.5 Tm (Not C: Pulmonary and systemic circuits retain very different resistance values.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 202.1 Tm (Source: lecture-6:Q59 | 45; Chapter 27 ?4) Tj ET
