BT /F2 12.0 Tf 1 1 1 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 815.0 Tm (DOMiNANCE / CVS PHYSIOLOGY) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 1 1 1 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 798.0 Tm (MOCK 04 | 60Q | EXPLANATION GUIDE | 90 MIN) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 745.0 Tm (44. V2 has been placed correctly and V4 is at its usual site. Where should V3 be attached?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 728.3 Tm (Answer: D. Halfway between the V2 and V4 electrodes.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 712.0 Tm (Explanation: V3 is placed between the already positioned V2 and V4, so the anatomical line between those contacts) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 698.9 Tm (determines its location.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 682.8 Tm (Not A: The midaxillary line at V4 level is used for V6.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 669.4 Tm (Not B: The anterior axillary line at V4 level is used for V5.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 656.0 Tm (Not C: The fourth right parasternal position belongs to V1.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 642.6 Tm (Source: lecture-7:Q12 | 5; Chapter 6 ?3) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 619.4 Tm (45. Using Bazett's formula, QTc = QT/?RR, what is QTc when QT is 0.36 s and RR is 0.81 s?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 602.7 Tm (Answer: D. 0.40 s, because QT is divided by 0.90.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 586.4 Tm (Explanation: Since ?0.81 = 0.90, Bazett's correction is 0.36/0.90 = 0.40 s. The correction adjusts the observed QT) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 573.3 Tm (for heart rate.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 557.2 Tm (Not A: Multiplying by RR is not Bazett's rate-correction formula.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 543.8 Tm (Not B: QT should be divided by ?RR, not multiplied by it.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 530.4 Tm (Not C: Adding QT and RR has no role in the stated equation.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 517.0 Tm (Source: lecture-7:Q32 | 12) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 493.8 Tm (46. Where is the largest controllable fall in systemic vascular pressure normally produced?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 477.1 Tm (Answer: C. Across resistance arterioles.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 460.8 Tm (Explanation: Small arteries and arterioles provide substantial resistance, producing a major pressure drop from) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 447.7 Tm (arterial to capillary levels.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 431.6 Tm (Not A: Large veins carry blood at low pressure and resistance.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 418.2 Tm (Not B: Right atrial pressure is low but does not create the main distributed arterial drop.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 404.8 Tm (Not D: Large elastic arteries conduct flow with relatively small resistance.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 391.4 Tm (Source: circulation-1:Q65 | 57, 61, 76; Chapter 10 ?9) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 368.2 Tm (47. Why does the sinoatrial node ordinarily set the heart's rhythm rather than latent pacemakers?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 351.5 Tm (Answer: B. It has the fastest intrinsic depolarisation rate.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 335.2 Tm (Explanation: The SA node normally reaches threshold fastest and overdrive-suppresses slower latent pacemakers.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 322.1 Tm (Faster SA-node impulses normally suppress slower escape pacemakers.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 306.0 Tm (Not A: Action potential duration is not the primary reason for the fastest spontaneous rate.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 292.6 Tm (Not C: Coronary perfusion is not determined by pulmonary venous timing.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 279.2 Tm (Not D: Pacemaker dominance does not depend on muscle mass.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 265.8 Tm (Source: circulation-1:Q12 | 7-9; Chapter 3 ?3) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 242.6 Tm (48. Which sequence correctly traces normal electrical conduction from atria into ventricular myocardium?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 225.9 Tm (Answer: B. SA node -> AV node -> His bundle -> Purkinje fibres.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 209.6 Tm (Explanation: The SA node initiates impulses that reach the AV node, traverse the His-bundle branches and Purkinje) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 196.5 Tm (system, then depolarise ventricles.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 180.4 Tm (Not A: Purkinje fibres come after the AV-His pathway.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 167.0 Tm (Not C: The SA node normally initiates atrial excitation.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 153.6 Tm (Not D: Ventricular Purkinje fibres conduct after, not before, atrial activation.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 140.2 Tm (Source: circulation-1:Q11 | 7-10) Tj ET
