BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 745.0 Tm (49. Why can coordinated cardiac pumping continue without a new external nerve impulse for every beat?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 728.3 Tm (Answer: B. Intrinsic pacemaker cells generate rhythmic impulses.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 712.0 Tm (Explanation: Specialised cardiac pacemaker cells depolarise spontaneously; autonomic nerves modulate rather than) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 698.9 Tm (initiate each heartbeat.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 682.8 Tm (Not A: Atrial conduction is not limited to expiration.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 669.4 Tm (Not C: The SA node produces electrical impulses, not direct ejection pressure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 656.0 Tm (Not D: Some conducting cells possess spontaneous automaticity.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 642.6 Tm (Source: circulation-1:Q4 | 7-10) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 619.4 Tm (50. A patient has near-normal resting cardiac output but very high ventricular filling pressures and) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 605.7 Tm (pulmonary congestion. Which statement fits best?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 589.0 Tm (Answer: A. Heart failure can occur with normal output.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 572.7 Tm (Explanation: Heart failure includes states where output is maintained only at abnormally high filling pressures,) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 559.6 Tm (causing congestion.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 543.5 Tm (Not B: High filling pressures can be evidence of dysfunction rather than normality.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 530.1 Tm (Not C: Diastolic failure can occur with preserved ejection fraction.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 516.7 Tm (Not D: A near-normal output can be maintained at harmful filling pressures.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 503.3 Tm (Source: circulation-3:Q47 | Circulation 3, slide 31; Chapter 21 ?1) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 480.1 Tm (51. What event ends isovolumetric contraction and begins ventricular ejection?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 463.4 Tm (Answer: D. Ventricular pressure exceeds the outflow artery pressure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 447.1 Tm (Explanation: Semilunar valves open when ventricular pressure exceeds aortic or pulmonary arterial pressure. The) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 434.0 Tm (pressure reversal is the immediate mechanical trigger for valve opening.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 417.9 Tm (Not A: This pressure relation opens the AV valves during filling.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 404.5 Tm (Not B: Atrial systole precedes isovolumetric contraction.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 391.1 Tm (Not C: Lower ventricular than aortic pressure holds the aortic valve closed.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 377.7 Tm (Source: circulation-1:Q20 | 14-17) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 354.5 Tm (52. Which pressure difference primarily drives blood back to the right atrium from the body?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 337.8 Tm (Answer: C. Systemic filling pressure minus right atrial pressure.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 321.5 Tm (Explanation: Venous return depends on the upstream systemic filling pressure relative to right atrial pressure and) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 308.4 Tm (the resistance to venous return.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 292.3 Tm (Not A: Aortic-right ventricular systolic pressure is not the venous return driving gradient.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 278.9 Tm (Not B: That difference is a pulse-pressure-related comparison.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 265.5 Tm (Not D: Pulmonary venous pressure does not define systemic venous return.) Tj ET
BT /F1 7.2 Tf 0.43 0.45 0.49 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 252.1 Tm (Source: circulation-1:Q74 | 97; Chapter 12 ?3) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.7 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 228.9 Tm (53. What can follow when the right ventricle fails to deliver adequate forward flow to the lungs?) Tj ET
BT /F2 9.3 Tf 0.1 0.33 0.58 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 212.2 Tm (Answer: B. Less blood reaches the left heart for systemic output.) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 195.9 Tm (Explanation: Reduced right ventricular output limits pulmonary flow and subsequent return to the left atrium,) Tj ET
BT /F1 9.1 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 182.8 Tm (compromising left-sided filling and delivery.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 166.7 Tm (Not A: The right heart cannot deliver excess flow when severely failing.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 153.3 Tm (Not C: Reduced pulmonary transit cannot raise systemic oxygen delivery.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.4 Tf 0.16 0.18 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 48.0 139.9 Tm (Not D: Less pulmonary venous return tends to decrease left-sided filling.) Tj ET
